alidation.py:learning_curve`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1351 | def learning_curve(\n1352 |     estimator,\n1353 |     X,\n1354 |     y,\n1355 |     *,\n1356 |     groups=None,\n1357 |     train_sizes=np.linspace(0.1, 1.0, 5),\n1358 |     cv=None,\n1359 |     scoring=None,\n1360 |     exploit_incremental_learning=False,\n1361 |     n_jobs=None,\n1362 |     pre_dispatch=\"all\",\n1363 |     verbose=0,\n1364 |     shuffle=False,\n1365 |     random_state=None,\n1366 |     error_score=np.nan,\n1367 |     return_times=False,\n1368 |     fit_params=None,\n1369 | ):\n1370 |     \"\"\"Learning curve.\n1371 | \n1372 |     Determines cross-validated training and test scores for different training\n1373 |     set sizes.\n1374 | \n1375 |     A cross-validation generator splits the whole dataset k times in training\n1376 |     and test data. Subsets of the training set with varying sizes will be used\n1377 |     to train the estimator and a score for each training subset size and the\n1378 |     test set will be computed. Afterwards, the scores will be averaged over\n1379 |     all k runs for each training subset size.\n1380 | \n1381 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <learning_curve>`.\n1382 | \n1383 |     Parameters\n1384 |     ----------\n1385 |     estimator : object type that implements the \"fit\" and \"predict\" methods\n1386 |         An object of that type which is cloned for each validation.\n1387 | \n1388 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1389 |         Training vector, where `n_samples` is the number of samples and\n1390 |         `n_features` is the number of features.\n1391 | \n1392 |     y : array-like of shape (n_samples,) or (n_samples, n_outputs)\n1393 |         Target relative to X for classification or regression;\n1394 |         None for unsupervised learning.\n1395 | \n1396 |     groups : array-like of  shape (n_samples,), default=None\n1397 |         Group labels for the samples used while splitting the dataset into\n1398 |         train/test set. Only used in conjunction with a \"Group\" :term:`cv`\n1399 |         instance (e.g., :class:`GroupKFold`).\n1400 | \n1401 |     train_sizes : array-like of shape (n_ticks,), \\\n1402 |             default=np.linspace(0.1, 1.0, 5)\n1403 |         Relative or absolute numbers of training examples that will be used to\n1404 |         generate the learning curve. If the dtype is float, it is regarded as a\n1405 |         fraction of the maximum size of the training set (that is determined\n1406 |         by the selected validation method), i.e. it has to be within (0, 1].\n1407 |         Otherwise it is interpreted as absolute sizes of the training sets.\n1408 |         Note that for classification the number of samples usually have to\n1409 |         be big enough to contain at least one sample from each class.\n1410 | \n1411 |     cv : int, cross-validation generator or an iterable, default=None\n1412 |         Determines the cross-validation splitting strategy.\n1413 |         Possible inputs for cv are:\n1414 | \n1415 |         - None, to use the default 5-fold cross validation,\n1416 |         - int, to specify the number of folds in a `(Stratified)KFold`,\n1417 |         - :term:`CV splitter`,\n1418 |         - An iterable yielding (train, test) splits as arrays of indices.\n1419 | \n1420 |         For int/None inputs, if the estimator is a classifier and ``y`` is\n1421 |         either binary or multiclass, :class:`StratifiedKFold` is used. In all\n1422 |         other cases, :class:`KFold` is used. These splitters are instantiated\n1423 |         with `shuffle=False` so the splits will be the same across calls.\n1424 | \n1425 |         Refer :ref:`User Guide <cross_validation>` for the various\n1426 |         cross-validation strategies that can be used here.\n1427 | \n1428 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.22\n1429 |             ``cv`` default value if None changed from 3-fold to 5-fold.\n1430 | \n1431 |     scoring : str or callable, default=None\n1432 |         A str (see model evaluation documentation) or\n1433 |         a scorer callable object / function with signature\n1434 |         ``scorer(estimator, X, y)``.\n1435 | \n1436 |     exploit_incremental_learning : bool, default=False\n1437 |         If the estimator supports incremental learning, this will be\n1438 |         used to speed up fitting for different training set sizes.\n1439 | \n1440 |     n_jobs : int, default=None\n1441 |         Number of jobs to run in parallel. Training the estimator and computing\n1442 |         the score are parallelized over the different training and test sets.\n1443 |         ``None`` means 1 unless in a :obj:`joblib.parallel_backend` context.\n1444 |         ``-1`` means using all processors. See :term:`Glossary <n_jobs>`\n1445 |         for more details.\n1446 | \n1447 |     pre_dispatch : int or str, default='all'\n1448 |         Number of predispatched jobs for parallel execution (default is\n1449 |         all). The option can reduce the allocated memory. The str can\n1450 |         be an expression like '2*n_jobs'.\n1451 | \n1452 |     verbose : int, default=0\n1453 |         Controls the verbosity: the higher, the more messages.\n1454 | \n1455 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n1456 |         Whether to shuffle training data before taking prefixes of it\n1457 |         based on``train_sizes``.\n1458 | \n1459 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1460 |         Used when ``shuffle`` is True. Pass an int for reproducible\n1461 |         output across multiple function calls.\n1462 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1463 | \n1464 |     error_score : 'raise' or numeric, default=np.nan\n1465 |         Value to assign to the score if an error occurs in estimator fitting.\n1466 |         If set to 'raise', the error is raised.\n1467 |         If a numeric value is given, FitFailedWarning is raised.\n1468 | \n1469 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n1470 | \n1471 |     return_times : bool, default=False\n1472 |         Whether to return the fit and score times.\n1473 | \n1474 |     fit_params : dict, default=None\n1475 |         Parameters to pass to the fit method of the estimator.\n1476 | \n1477 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n1478 | \n1479 |     Returns\n1480 |     -------\n1481 |     train_sizes_abs : array of shape (n_unique_ticks,)\n1482 |         Numbers of training examples that has been used to generate the\n1483 |         learning curve. Note that the number of ticks might be less\n1484 |         than n_ticks because duplicate entries will be removed.\n1485 | \n1486 |     train_scores : array of shape (n_ticks, n_cv_folds)\n1487 |         Scores on training sets.\n1488 | \n1489 |     test_scores : array of shape (n_ticks, n_cv_folds)\n1490 |         Scores on test set.\n1491 | \n1492 |     fit_times : array of shape (n_ticks, n_cv_folds)\n1493 |         Times spent for fitting in seconds. Only present if ``return_times``\n1494 |         is True.\n1495 | \n1496 |     score_times : array of shape (n_ticks, n_cv_folds)\n1497 |         Times spent for scoring in seconds. Only present if ``return_times``\n1498 |         is True.\n1499 | \n1500 |     Notes\n1501 |     -----\n1502 |     See :ref:`examples/model_selection/plot_learning_curve.py\n1503 |     <sphx_glr_auto_examples_model_selection_plot_learning_curve.py>`\n1504 |     \"\"\"\n1505 |     if exploit_incremental_learning and not hasattr(estimator, \"partial_fit\"):\n1506 |         raise ValueError(\n1507 |             \"An estimator must support the partial_fit interface \"\n1508 |             \"to exploit incremental learning\"\n1509 |         )\n1510 |     X, y, groups = indexable(X, y, groups)\n1511 | \n1512 |     cv = check_cv(cv, y, classifier=is_classifier(estimator))\n1513 |     # Store it as list as we will be iterating over the list multiple times\n1514 |     cv_iter = list(cv.split(X, y, groups))\n1515 | \n1516 |     scorer = check_scoring(estimator, scoring=scoring)\n1517 | \n1518 |     n_max_training_samples = len(cv_iter[0][0])\n1519 |     # Because the lengths of folds can be significantly different, it is\n1520 |     # not guaranteed that we use all of the available training data when we\n1521 |     # use the first 'n_max_training_samples' samples.\n1522 |     train_sizes_abs = _translate_train_sizes(train_sizes, n_max_training_samples)\n1523 |     n_unique_ticks = train_sizes_abs.shape[0]\n1524 |     if verbose > 0:\n1525 |         print(\"[learning_curve] Training set sizes: \" + str(train_sizes_abs))\n1526 | \n1527 |     parallel = Parallel(n_jobs=n_jobs, pre_dispatch=pre_dispatch, verbose=verbose)\n1528 | \n1529 |     if shuffle:\n1530 |         rng = check_random_state(random_state)\n1531 |         cv_iter = ((rng.permutation(train), test) for train, test in cv_iter)\n1532 | \n1533 |     if exploit_incremental_learning:\n1534 |         classes = np.unique(y) if is_classifier(estimator) else None\n1535 |         out = parallel(\n1536 |             delayed(_incremental_fit_estimator)(\n1537 |                 clone(estimator),\n1538 |                 X,\n1539 |                 y,\n1540 |                 classes,\n1541 |                 train,\n1542 |                 test,\n1543 |                 train_sizes_abs,\n1544 |                 scorer,\n1545 |                 verbose,\n1546 |                 return_times,\n1547 |                 error_score=error_score,\n1548 |                 fit_params=fit_params,\n1549 |             )\n1550 |             for train, test in cv_iter\n1551 |         )\n1552 |         out = np.asarray(out).transpose((2, 1, 0))\n1553 |     else:\n1554 |         train_test_proportions = []\n1555 |         for train, test in cv_iter:\n1556 |             for n_train_samples in train_sizes_abs:\n1557 |                 train_test_proportions.append((train[:n_train_samples], test))\n1558 | \n1559 |         results = parallel(\n1560 |             delayed(_fit_and_score)(\n1561 |                 clone(estimator),\n1562 |                 X,\n1563 |                 y,\n1564 |                 scorer,\n1565 |                 train,\n1566 |                 test,\n1567 |                 verbose,\n1568 |                 parameters=None,\n1569 |                 fit_params=fit_params,\n1570 |                 return_train_score=True,\n1571 |                 error_score=error_score,\n1572 |                 return_times=return_times,\n1573 |             )\n1574 |             for train, test in train_test_proportions\n1575 |         )\n1576 |         results = _aggregate_score_dicts(results)\n1577 |         train_scores = results[\"train_scores\"].reshape(-1, n_unique_ticks).T\n1578 |         test_scores = results[\"test_scores\"].reshape(-1, n_unique_ticks).T\n1579 |         out = [train_scores, test_scores]\n1580 | \n1581 |         if return_times:\n1582 |             fit_times = results[\"fit_time\"].reshape(-1, n_unique_ticks).T\n1583 |             score_times = results[\"score_time\"].reshape(-1, n_unique_ticks).T\n1584 |             out.extend([fit_times, score_times])\n1585 | \n1586 |     ret = train_sizes_abs, out[0], out[1]\n1587 | \n1588 |     if return_times:\n1589 |         ret = ret + (out[2], out[3])\n1590 | \n1591 |     return ret\n```\n\nFound function `asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/utils.py:make_dict_learning_scorers`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n24 | def make_dict_learning_scorers(caller):\n25 |     caller.train_scorer = lambda _, __: (\n26 |         neg_mean_data_error(\n27 |             caller.X, caller.estimator.transform(caller.X), caller.estimator.components_\n28 |         )\n29 |     )\n30 |     caller.test_scorer = lambda _, __: (\n31 |         neg_mean_data_error(\n32 |             caller.X_val,\n33 |             caller.estimator.transform(caller.X_val),\n34 |             caller.estimator.components_,\n35 |         )\n36 |     )\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/linear_model/_stoch