irection. In the\n5012 |             *y*-direction, counting is done along vertically aligned\n5013 |             hexagons, not along the zig-zag chains of hexagons; see the\n5014 |             following illustration.\n5015 | \n5016 |             .. plot::\n5017 | \n5018 |                import numpy\n5019 |                import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n5020 | \n5021 |                np.random.seed(19680801)\n5022 |                n= 300\n5023 |                x = np.random.standard_normal(n)\n5024 |                y = np.random.standard_normal(n)\n5025 | \n5026 |                fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(4, 4))\n5027 |                h = ax.hexbin(x, y, gridsize=(5, 3))\n5028 |                hx, hy = h.get_offsets().T\n5029 |                ax.plot(hx[24::3], hy[24::3], 'ro-')\n5030 |                ax.plot(hx[-3:], hy[-3:], 'ro-')\n5031 |                ax.set_title('gridsize=(5, 3)')\n5032 |                ax.axis('off')\n5033 | \n5034 |             To get approximately regular hexagons, choose\n5035 |             :math:`n_x = \\\\sqrt{3}\\\\,n_y`.\n5036 | \n5037 |         bins : 'log' or int or sequence, default: None\n5038 |             Discretization of the hexagon values.\n5039 | \n5040 |             - If *None*, no binning is applied; the color of each hexagon\n5041 |               directly corresponds to its count value.\n5042 |             - If 'log', use a logarithmic scale for the colormap.\n5043 |               Internally, :math:`log_{10}(i+1)` is used to determine the\n5044 |               hexagon color. This is equivalent to ``norm=LogNorm()``.\n5045 |             - If an integer, divide the counts in the specified number\n5046 |               of bins, and color the hexagons accordingly.\n5047 |             - If a sequence of values, the values of the lower bound of\n5048 |               the bins to be used.\n5049 | \n5050 |         xscale : {'linear', 'log'}, default: 'linear'\n5051 |             Use a linear or log10 scale on the horizontal axis.\n5052 | \n5053 |         yscale : {'linear', 'log'}, default: 'linear'\n5054 |             Use a linear or log10 scale on the vertical axis.\n5055 | \n5056 |         mincnt : int >= 0, default: *None*\n5057 |             If not *None*, only display cells with at least *mincnt*\n5058 |             number of points in the cell.\n5059 | \n5060 |         marginals : bool, default: *False*\n5061 |             If marginals is *True*, plot the marginal density as\n5062 |             colormapped rectangles along the bottom of the x-axis and\n5063 |             left of the y-axis.\n5064 | \n5065 |         extent : 4-tuple of float, default: *None*\n5066 |             The limits of the bins (xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax).\n5067 |             The default assigns the limits based on\n5068 |             *gridsize*, *x*, *y*, *xscale* and *yscale*.\n5069 | \n5070 |             If *xscale* or *yscale* is set to 'log', the limits are\n5071 |             expected to be the exponent for a power of 10. E.g. for\n5072 |             x-limits of 1 and 50 in 'linear' scale and y-limits\n5073 |             of 10 and 1000 in 'log' scale, enter (1, 50, 1, 3).\n5074 | \n5075 |         Returns\n5076 |         -------\n5077 |         `~matplotlib.collections.PolyCollection`\n5078 |             A `.PolyCollection` defining the hexagonal bins.\n5079 | \n5080 |             - `.PolyCollection.get_offsets` contains a Mx2 array containing\n5081 |               the x, y positions of the M hexagon centers in data coordinates.\n5082 |             - `.PolyCollection.get_array` contains the values of the M\n5083 |               hexagons.\n5084 | \n5085 |             If *marginals* is *True*, horizontal\n5086 |             bar and vertical bar (both PolyCollections) will be attached\n5087 |             to the return collection as attributes *hbar* and *vbar*.\n5088 | \n5089 |         Other Parameters\n5090 |         ----------------\n5091 |         %(cmap_doc)s\n5092 | \n5093 |         %(norm_doc)s\n5094 | \n5095 |         %(vmin_vmax_doc)s\n5096 | \n5097 |         alpha : float between 0 and 1, optional\n5098 |             The alpha blending value, between 0 (transparent) and 1 (opaque).\n5099 | \n5100 |         linewidths : float, default: *None*\n5101 |             If *None*, defaults to :rc:`patch.linewidth`.\n5102 | \n5103 |         edgecolors : {'face', 'none', *None*} or color, default: 'face'\n5104 |             The color of the hexagon edges. Possible values are:\n5105 | \n5106 |             - 'face': Draw the edges in the same color as the fill color.\n5107 |             - 'none': No edges are drawn. This can sometimes lead to unsightly\n5108 |               unpainted pixels between the hexagons.\n5109 |             - *None*: Draw outlines in the default color.\n5110 |             - An explicit color.\n5111 | \n5112 |         reduce_C_function : callable, default: `numpy.mean`\n5113 |             The function to aggregate *C* within the bins. It is ignored if\n5114 |             *C* is not given. This must have the signature::\n5115 | \n5116 |                 def reduce_C_function(C: array) -> float\n5117 | \n5118 |             Commonly used functions are:\n5119 | \n5120 |             - `numpy.mean`: average of the points\n5121 |             - `numpy.sum`: integral of the point values\n5122 |             - `numpy.amax`: value taken from the largest point\n5123 | \n5124 |             By default will only reduce cells with at least 1 point because some\n5125 |             reduction functions (such as `numpy.amax`) will error/warn with empty\n5126 |             input. Changing *mincnt* will adjust the cutoff, and if set to 0 will\n5127 |             pass empty input to the reduction function.\n5128 | \n5129 |         %(colorizer_doc)s\n5130 | \n5131 |         data : indexable object, optional\n5132 |             DATA_PARAMETER_PLACEHOLDER\n5133 | \n5134 |         **kwargs : `~matplotlib.collections.PolyCollection` properties\n5135 |             All other keyword arguments are passed on to `.PolyCollection`:\n5136 | \n5137 |             %(PolyCollection:kwdoc)s\n5138 | \n5139 |         See Also\n5140 |         --------\n5141 |         hist2d : 2D histogram rectangular bins\n5142 |         \"\"\"\n5143 |         self._process_unit_info([(\"x\", x), (\"y\", y)], kwargs, convert=False)\n5144 | \n5145 |         x, y, C = cbook.delete_masked_points(x, y, C)\n5146 | \n5147 |         # Set the size of the hexagon grid\n5148 |         if np.iterable(gridsize):\n5149 |             nx, ny = gridsize\n5150 |         else:\n5151 |             nx = gridsize\n5152 |             ny = int(nx / math.sqrt(3))\n5153 |         # Count the number of data in each hexagon\n5154 |         x = np.asarray(x, float)\n5155 |         y = np.asarray(y, float)\n5156 | \n5157 |         # Will be log()'d if necessary, and then rescaled.\n5158 |         tx = x\n5159 |         ty = y\n5160 | \n5161 |         if xscale == 'log':\n5162 |             if np.any(x <= 0.0):\n5163 |                 raise ValueError(\n5164 |                     \"x contains non-positive values, so cannot be log-scaled\")\n5165 |             tx = np.log10(tx)\n5166 |         if yscale == 'log':\n5167 |             if np.any(y <= 0.0):\n5168 |                 raise ValueError(\n5169 |                     \"y contains non-positive values, so cannot be log-scaled\")\n5170 |             ty = np.log10(ty)\n5171 |         if extent is not None:\n5172 |             xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax = extent\n5173 |             if xmin > xmax:\n5174 |                 raise ValueError(\"In extent, xmax must be greater than xmin\")\n5175 |             if ymin > ymax:\n5176 |                 raise ValueError(\"In extent, ymax must be greater than ymin\")\n5177 |         else:\n5178 |             xmin, xmax = (tx.min(), tx.max()) if len(x) else (0, 1)\n5179 |             ymin, ymax = (ty.min(), ty.max()) if len(y) else (0, 1)\n5180 | \n5181 |             # to avoid issues with singular data, expand the min/max pairs\n5182 |             xmin, xmax = mtransforms.nonsingular(xmin, xmax, expander=0.1)\n5183 |             ymin, ymax = mtransforms.nonsingular(ymin, ymax, expander=0.1)\n5184 | \n5185 |         nx1 = nx + 1\n5186 |         ny1 = ny + 1\n5187 |         nx2 = nx\n5188 |         ny2 = ny\n5189 |         n = nx1 * ny1 + nx2 * ny2\n5190 | \n5191 |         # In the x-direction, the hexagons exactly cover the region from\n5192 |         # xmin to xmax. Need some padding to avoid roundoff errors.\n5193 |         padding = 1.e-9 * (xmax - xmin)\n5194 |         xmin -= padding\n5195 |         xmax += padding\n5196 |         sx = (xmax - xmin) / nx\n5197 |         sy = (ymax - ymin) / ny\n5198 |         # Positions in hexagon index coordinates.\n5199 |         ix = (tx - xmin) / sx\n5200 |         iy = (ty - ymin) / sy\n5201 |         ix1 = np.round(ix).astype(int)\n5202 |         iy1 = np.round(iy).astype(int)\n5203 |         ix2 = np.floor(ix).astype(int)\n5204 |         iy2 = np.floor(iy).astype(int)\n5205 |         # flat indices, plus one so that out-of-range points go to position 0.\n5206 |         i1 = np.where((0 <= ix1) & (ix1 < nx1) & (0 <= iy1) & (iy1 < ny1),\n5207 |                       ix1 * ny1 + iy1 + 1, 0)\n5208 |         i2 = np.where((0 <= ix2) & (ix2 < nx2) & (0 <= iy2) & (iy2 < ny2),\n5209 |                       ix2 * ny2 + iy2 + 1, 0)\n5210 | \n5211 |         d1 = (ix - ix1) ** 2 + 3.0 * (iy - iy1) ** 2\n5212 |         d2 = (ix - ix2 - 0.5) ** 2 + 3.0 * (iy - iy2 - 0.5) ** 2\n5213 |         bdist = (d1 < d2)\n5214 | \n5215 |         if C is None:  # [1:] drops out-of-range points.\n5216 |             counts1 = np.bincount(i1[bdist], minlength=1 + nx1 * ny1)[1:]\n5217 |             counts2 = np.bincount(i2[~bdist], minlength=1 + nx2 * ny2)[1:]\n5218 |             accum = np.concatenate([counts1, counts2]).astype(float)\n5219 |             if mincnt is not None:\n5220 |                 accum[accum < mincnt] = np.nan\n5221 |             C = np.one